WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.748
Every question a program asks a database reads pages.

2
00:00:06.748 --> 00:00:09.421
Looking up one payment reads a few.

3
00:00:09.421 --> 00:00:11.942
A monthly report reads thousands.

4
00:00:11.942 --> 00:00:17.059
Reading one 8 KB page from a fast SSD takes about 100 microseconds.

5
00:00:17.059 --> 00:00:19.885
From memory, it takes well under one.

6
00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:26.144
And queries keep coming back to the same pages: today's payments, the table of card limits.

7
00:00:26.144 --> 00:00:27.813
So keep copies in memory.

8
00:00:27.813 --> 00:00:29.149
But memory is small.

9
00:00:29.149 --> 00:00:32.488
Which pages stay, and what happens when it's full?

10
00:00:32.833 --> 00:00:35.405
Picture yourself studying at a small desk.

11
00:00:35.405 --> 00:00:39.691
It holds four open books, and the rest sit on a shelf across the room.

12
00:00:39.691 --> 00:00:41.896
A glance at the desk takes a second.

13
00:00:41.896 --> 00:00:44.039
A walk to the shelf takes a minute.

14
00:00:44.167 --> 00:00:47.129
So you keep the books you're using on the desk.

15
00:00:47.129 --> 00:00:50.343
And when you need a fifth book, one has to go back.

16
00:00:50.343 --> 00:00:50.974
Which one?

17
00:00:50.974 --> 00:00:52.108
Hold that thought.

18
00:00:52.467 --> 00:00:53.475
Let's map it.

19
00:00:53.475 --> 00:00:55.259
The shelf is the drive.

20
00:00:55.259 --> 00:00:56.733
The desk is memory.

21
00:00:56.733 --> 00:00:58.595
And each book is a page.

22
00:00:58.733 --> 00:01:01.312
The picture breaks in one small way.

23
00:01:01.312 --> 00:01:03.675
A desk takes books of every size.

24
00:01:03.675 --> 00:01:09.548
In memory, every place holds exactly one 8 KB page, so any page fits in any place.

25
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:16.647
The first idea: a map in memory from each page number to that page's bytes, filled on every read.

26
00:01:16.647 --> 00:01:21.168
It works, until the database is bigger than memory, and most are.

27
00:01:21.168 --> 00:01:24.854
A 2 TB table won't fit in a server's 64 GB of memory.

28
00:01:24.854 --> 00:01:28.401
That desk would need to be the size of the library.

29
00:01:28.567 --> 00:01:30.520
So the cache needs a fixed size.

30
00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:33.145
And once it's full, something has to leave.

31
00:01:33.533 --> 00:01:38.463
So, four places on the desk, and a list of which page sits in which place.

32
00:01:38.463 --> 00:01:39.928
A query wants page 10.

33
00:01:39.928 --> 00:01:41.593
The list doesn't have it.

34
00:01:41.593 --> 00:01:46.723
So we walk to the shelf, read page 10 into place 0, and write it on the list.

35
00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:51.323
Pages 11, 12 and 13 fill the other places the same way.

36
00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:53.184
Now page 11 again.

37
00:01:53.184 --> 00:01:55.121
The list says place 1.

38
00:01:55.121 --> 00:01:56.441
No walk at all.

39
00:01:56.633 --> 00:01:57.837
These have names.

40
00:01:57.837 --> 00:02:01.305
The places are frames, each the size of one page.

41
00:02:01.305 --> 00:02:03.217
The list is the page table.

42
00:02:03.217 --> 00:02:08.951
Found on the list is a hit, and no input or output happens, no trip to the drive.

43
00:02:08.951 --> 00:02:10.366
Not found is a miss.

44
00:02:10.366 --> 00:02:12.844
The whole thing is the buffer pool.

45
00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:15.194
Now a query wants page 42.

46
00:02:15.194 --> 00:02:16.779
Every frame is full.

47
00:02:16.779 --> 00:02:18.761
What has to happen first?

48
00:02:18.761 --> 00:02:20.029
Pause and guess.

49
00:02:23.267 --> 00:02:24.742
A page has to leave.

50
00:02:24.742 --> 00:02:28.798
The pool picks a victim, a page to throw out, or evict.

51
00:02:28.798 --> 00:02:30.421
Say, for now, page 13.

52
00:02:30.421 --> 00:02:34.920
Then page 42 is read into its frame, and the list is updated.

53
00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:36.885
Which page should be the victim?

54
00:02:36.885 --> 00:02:40.511
That's the fifth book from the desk, and the rest of this lesson.

55
00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:47.949
PostgreSQL, a free database many companies run, calls its pool shared_buffers.

56
00:02:47.949 --> 00:02:56.444
By default it's 128 megabytes: 16,384 frames of 8 KB, shared by every program connected to it.

57
00:02:56.633 --> 00:03:01.883
In the Try it, a table of 200,000 payments takes 1,280 pages.

58
00:03:01.883 --> 00:03:06.099
We throw them out of the pool and count the rows.

59
00:03:06.099 --> 00:03:09.197
The first run reads all 1,280 pages.

60
00:03:09.197 --> 00:03:11.348
Run the same count again.

61
00:03:11.348 --> 00:03:12.811
What will it say?

62
00:03:12.811 --> 00:03:14.188
Pause and guess.

63
00:03:17.433 --> 00:03:20.265
1,280 hits, and no reads at all.

64
00:03:20.265 --> 00:03:27.168
One more honest detail: those first 1,280 reads took 0.15 milliseconds in all.

65
00:03:27.168 --> 00:03:35.309
The operating system kept its own copy of the pages in memory, so the SSD was never touched.

66
00:03:35.633 --> 00:03:38.915
So why not let the operating system cache everything?

67
00:03:38.915 --> 00:03:39.906
Some systems do.

68
00:03:39.906 --> 00:03:43.312
But the database knows three things the kernel doesn't.

69
00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:46.063
Which pages a query is using right now.

70
00:03:46.063 --> 00:03:51.650
That a changed page must not reach the drive before the log record that describes it.

71
00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:54.805
And that a big scan's pages won't be read again.

72
00:03:54.967 --> 00:04:01.004
A 2022 study measured databases that leave these choices to the kernel through mmap.

73
00:04:01.004 --> 00:04:04.886
Once memory filled up, they were 2 to 20 times slower.

74
00:04:04.886 --> 00:04:06.467
The kernel means well.

75
00:04:06.467 --> 00:04:08.839
It just can't see inside a query.

76
00:04:09.167 --> 00:04:16.541
So: keep a fixed number of page-sized frames in memory, and a page table that says which page is in which frame.

77
00:04:16.541 --> 00:04:17.857
A hit costs nothing.

78
00:04:17.857 --> 00:04:21.347
A miss costs a trip to the drive, and maybe a victim.

79
00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:23.228
One quick check.

80
00:04:23.228 --> 00:04:27.156
The frames now hold pages 10, 11, 12 and 42.

81
00:04:27.156 --> 00:04:30.370
A query reads page 11, then page 13.

82
00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:32.245
Which read is a miss?

83
00:04:32.245 --> 00:04:33.673
Pause and think.

84
00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:37.565
Page 13.

85
00:04:37.565 --> 00:04:41.309
It was the victim, so it's back on the shelf.

86
00:04:41.309 --> 00:04:42.723
Page 11 is a hit.
